\toprule
\textbf{System} & \textbf{Validators / Entities} & \textbf{HHI (approx.)} & \textbf{Nakamoto Coeff.} \\
\midrule
Polkadot (NPoS, \textasciitilde{}300 validators) & \textasciitilde{}300 (near-equal stake) & \textasciitilde{}33 & \textasciitilde{}80--100 \\
Ethereum PoS & \textasciitilde{}900K validators, but concentrated in \textasciitilde{}5 staking providers & \textasciitilde{}1,200--2,000 & \textasciitilde{}5--7 \\
Solana & \textasciitilde{}1,900 validators, top-heavy stake & \textasciitilde{}200--500 & \textasciitilde{}19--25 \\
Centralised DRM (e.g., Spotify) & 1 entity & 10,000 & 1 \\
US DOJ ``unconcentrated'' threshold & --- & <1,500 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
}
\end{table}

\textbf{Conclusion:} The CCRMS parachain, by inheriting Polkadot's shared security, achieves an HHI of \textasciitilde{}33 --- well below the 1,500 threshold proposed in the original methodology (SQ2) and orders of magnitude below centralised alternatives. This validates the decentralisation objective without requiring a local multi-validator experiment.

\textbf{Note:} These figures use Polkadot mainnet data (\textasciitilde{}300 validators as of early 2026). The thesis's local testnet (2 validators, HHI = 5,000) is not representative of production decentralisation --- the relevant metric is the relay chain's validator set that the parachain would inherit upon deployment.

